\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Gaussian matter and fixed-length sign matter}
\date{2026-11-19}

\p{The [joint gauge and real-matter measure](fdip-0076) integrates an
unbounded real scalar using Euclidean volume. Its conditional matter
probability law is Gaussian, and integrating the scalar gives the
determinant weight of \ref{fdip-0077}. The sign scalar in
\ref{fdip-010H} takes two values. Its connection weight is obtained
by summing their products over the site assignments.}

\p{\citet{§3, p. 5, equations (12)-(13)}{gliozzi2004overlap}
use a cubic lattice with two spatial dimensions and one time
dimension. Their spins and links are signs. In the notation of
\ref{fdip-010B}, each edge contributes
#{\ue^{w_e\phi_{h(e)}U_e\phi_{s(e)}}} and each square contributes
#{\ue^{\beta U_f}}, with a uniform hopping coupling and plaquette
coupling #{\beta}. Their partition integral uses the product of
these weights. The finite loop comparison in \ref{fdip-0079}
identifies the further volume and loop estimates that a screening
claim would require.}
